@flexiarg institutions/conflict-resolution-mechanisms
@title Conflict-Resolution Mechanisms
@keywords conflict resolution, arenas, cost, locality, Ostrom 6, common-pool resources
@audience institution designers, commons researchers
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 6, wording as in Cox, Arnold and Tomas (2010), "A review of design principles for community-based natural resource management", Ecology and Society 15(4):38, quoted in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016) Table 1. Original source Ostrom 1990, Governing the Commons. Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to vet against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010 before relying on non-quoted text
@references [institutions/ARGUMENT creativity-commons/low-cost-local-conflict-arenas]

! conclusion:
  "Appropriators and their officials have rapid access to low-cost local arenas to resolve conflicts among appropriators or between appropriators and officials."

  + context:
    Conflicts arise both among users and between users and the officials who apply the rules.

  + IF:
    Users interpret rules differently or their uses collide.

  + HOWEVER:
    Local arenas can be captured by local factions; some conflicts need an authority beyond the group.

  + THEN:
    Provide quick, cheap, local places to settle conflicts among users and between users and officials.

  + BECAUSE:
    Conflicts that cannot be settled cheaply and quickly escalate or fester, and undermine the rules themselves.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Vet the HOWEVER and BECAUSE against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010; only the conclusion is quoted.]
    next[Creativity-commons transposition: creativity-commons/low-cost-local-conflict-arenas.]
